# numpy >= 2.4 / Python 3.11: building an ndarray from a Python sequence routes
# through PyArray_AssignFromCache -> <dtype>_setitem, whose vectorised store
# branches on padding bytes Valgrind flags as uninitialised. Harmless numpy
# internal (the model runs to completion); not a DARTS bug. Surfaced by the
# Python-3.11 / numpy-2.4.x migration. Wildcard fun:*_setitem covers
# DOUBLE_/FLOAT_/LONG_/... dtype variants.
{
   numpy-AssignFromCache-setitem-Cond
   Memcheck:Cond
   fun:*_setitem
   fun:PyArray_AssignFromCache_Recursive
   ...
}
{
   numpy-AssignFromCache-setitem-Value8
   Memcheck:Value8
   fun:*_setitem
   fun:PyArray_AssignFromCache_Recursive
   ...
}
# Catch-all for the remaining numpy-internal uninitialised-value false positives
# under Python 3.11 / numpy >= 2.4 / Valgrind 3.24 (the manylinux wheel on the CI
# image surfaces a few more contexts in both the main process and the numpy-import
# child than the setitem case above). These match only errors whose *innermost*
# frame is inside a numpy shared object (numpy/_core/_multiarray_umath, the CPU
# dispatch modules, and the bundled BLAS in numpy.libs), so genuine DARTS bugs
# (engines/discretizer/interpolators/libIPhreeqc .so) are still reported.
{
   numpy-internal-cond
   Memcheck:Cond
   obj:*/numpy/*.so
}
{
   numpy-internal-value8
   Memcheck:Value8
   obj:*/numpy/*.so
}
{
   numpy-libs-cond
   Memcheck:Cond
   obj:*/numpy.libs/*
}
{
   numpy-libs-value8
   Memcheck:Value8
   obj:*/numpy.libs/*
}
